TRAVELTIME
++++++++++

SUMMARY
-------
Computes traveltime of selected phases for pre-defined velocity models.
To get the Signal-Stacking Subprocess version of traveltime, go to SSSTRAVELTIME_.


SYNTAX
------
::

   TRAVELTIME {MODEL string} {PICKS number}
       {PHASE phase list}  {VERBOSE | QUIET} {M | KM}
       {ONLINE} {ONRECORD ON|OFF}

INPUT
-----

   :MODEL: iasp91, ak135

   :PICKS: There are a total of 10 time picks in the SAC header: t0 to t9.  If
     the number is n (0<=n<=9), the first phase will be at Tn. If PICKS is not
     included among the command-line options, VERBOSE will be turned on and the
     phase arrival times will be displayed but will not be put in the header.
     The default is not to have PICKS on, so no arrival times will be put in the
     header.  If PICKS is on by default for a later run, it cannot be turned off
     for subsequent runs.

   :PHASE: List of phases for which times are picked and displayed. If PICKS n
     is among the options, the phase arrivala times and their labels will be
     added to the header starting at Tn.  If n is 0, one can have up to 10
     phases, if n is 8, one can have at most 2.  Phase names may be case
     sensitive.  Using the CLEAR keyword to start the phase list, clears the
     list of phases from previous runs with the same dataset.

   :VERBOSE | QUIET:  If VERBOSE is among the options in the TRAVELTIME command
     line, phase arrival times are displayed relative to both the origin time
     (O) and the first-point time (B).  If QUIET is among the options in the
     TRAVELTIME command line, VERBOSE is turned off (if it was on) and nothing
     is displayed.

   :M | KM:  If M is among the options in the command line, SAC interprets EVDP
     as being in meters.  If KM is displayed, EVDP is interpreted as being in
     kilometers. (Historically, M was the default.)

   :ONLINE: Retrieve traveltimes from the IRIS Traveltime Web Service.  This
     uses Crotwell's TauP Toolkit internally. In addition to iasp91 and ak135,
     it includes model prem.  Also, it includes additional phases such as ttp,
     ts, ttp+, tts+, ttbasic, ttall.

   :ONRECORD ON|OFF: Filters traveltimes only within the time range defined
     by the data's Begin and End values. OFF is the Default

   :DEFAULT VALUES: MODEL iasp91 KM PHASE P S Pn Pg Sn Sg

DESCRIPTION
-----------
   All waveforms in memory must have event and station locations defined as well
   as the event depth and the origin time.

   This command calculates traveltimes using the iaspei-tau procedures developed
   for models iasp91 and ak135. For more information on this package, go to the
   iaspei-tau link at the reference given below. The phase picks
   are stored in the SAC header for all files in memory in header variables Tn,
   where n is in the range 0 to 9. The times are calculated relative to the
   origin time (O) but the displayed and stored Tn times are relative to the
   first-point time (B).

   The traveltime tables use the stored degree-distance measure (GCARC), which
   is calculated from the event and station latitude and longitudes using
   spherical-triangle geometry after converting geographic latitudes to
   geocentric.

   To find out all the possible phases that could be be visible on a
   seismogram according to TRAVELYIM, it is best to use *phase all*::

     SAC> read seismogram
     SAC> traveltime phase all verbose

   As discussed in DATAGEN_, a set of seismograms have been added that can be
   used fortesting and demonstrations.  For example::

     SAC> datagen sub teleseis sakhalin/BLA.dLHR
     SAC> lh b e o gcarc evdp
        b = 5.911000e+02                        e = 1.499100e+03
        o = 0.000000e+00                    gcarc = 8.627435e+01
        evdp = 6.110000e+02
     SAC> traveltime phase all verbose
        traveltime: depth: 611.000 km
        ...
        traveltime: SKSac    at 1265.9326 s [ t = 1265.9326 s ]
        ...
     SAC> traveltime phase SKS verbose
        traveltime: depth: 611.000 km
        traveltime: P        at 698.2086 s [ t = 698.2086 s ]
        traveltime: SKSac    at 1265.9326 s [ t = 1265.9326 s ]

   So, for this phase SKSac is in the time range for the seismograms.  Prior to
   v102.0, the command *traveltime phase SKS verbose* would not have worked.

   For that event-station, there are XX arrivals in the time rang (b) to (E),
   most of them are not observable.  As only 10 picks can be plotted, it is best
   to first run  traveltime phase all verbose to find possible arrivals but for
   picks to be plotted, just include that subset.  For that event-station
   seismogram, the following works well::

     SAC> datagen sub teleseis sakhalin/BLA.dLHR
     SAC> traveltime  quiet picks 0 phase P pP PP sP ScP PcS S SPn PKiKP SKS
     SAC> lh picks
        NPTS = 909                        B = 5.911000e+02
        E = 1.499100e+03                  OMARKER = 0
        T0MARKER = 698.21          (P)         T1MARKER = 828.45           (pP)
        T2MARKER = 910.6           (PP)        T3MARKER = 889.88           (sP)
        T4MARKER = 1283.5          (S)         T5MARKER = 1347.9           (SPn)
        T6MARKER = 1000.7        (PKiKP)     T7MARKER = 1265.9           (SKSac)
        KZDATE = MAY 12 (132), 1990             KZTIME = 04:50:08.900

   One advantage of TRAVELTIME in SAC over programs like TTIMES is the ability
   to make plots..  For historic reasons, TRAVELTIME is in the main commands
   dataset (this one) and in SSS.  Here a good example as to how to make plots
   is to use *P1* after reading in seismograms from a set of stations for the
   same event.  For this we use a subset of the sakhalin dataset.  *P1* plots
   traes from top to bottom in the order read i, so a set has been renamed so
   that the order will be in distance::

     SAC> datagen sub teleseis sakhalin/*.R
         /usr/local/sac/aux/datagen/teleseis/sakhalin/A1KEV.R ...A2KIP.R
         ...A3PAS.R ...A4BLA.R
     SAC> traveltime  quiet picks 0 phase P pP PP sP ScP PcS S SPn PKiKP SKS
     SAC> FILEID LOCATION UL TYPE LIST KSTCMP
     SAC> TITLE 'Sakhalin Island Event: &1,KZDATE&, Depth is &1,EVDP& KM'
     SAC> p1
     SAC> save sakhalin-picks.pdf

.. figure:: ./sakhalin-picks.png
   :width: 90%

   In SSSTRAVELTIME_ a different plot is included.

   For historical reason, SAC waveforms produced by RDSEED had EVDP in meters.
   Beginning with v101.5, the default for EVDP was changed to  kilometers, but
   as old waveforms may have EVDP in meters, the command-line option M means the
   input EVDP for all files in memory  have EVDP inmeters.  In RDSEED v5.2 or
   later, EVDEP is in kilometers.   For all runs of TRAVELTIME, the depth is
   printed to the screen so it should be obvious whether meters or kilometers
   were used

ONLINE OPTION
-------------

  The online option requests traveltime picks from the IRIS Traveltime Web
  Service, which uses Crotwell's TauP Toolkit:   All options are the same with
  the exception of a much broader array of traveltime that are able to be
  calculated and model PREM is an option.  To see the phase options, one should
  look at the TauP documentation at the reference below.  An advantage of
  using SAC instead of just using the IRIS Traveltime Web Service directly or T
  TauP directly is the ability to make seismogram plots.  As an example we
  repeat a run from above but use the PREM model::

     SAC> datagen sub teleseis sakhalin/BLA.dLHR
     SAC> traveltime online model prem quiet picks 0 phase P pP PP sP ScP PcS S
          SPn PKiKP SKS
     SAC> lh picks
       NPTS = 909                                 B = 5.911000e+02
       E = 1.499100e+03                  OMARKER = 0
       T0MARKER = 697.05           (P)       T1MARKER = 998.83           (PKiKP)
       T2MARKER = 909.41           (PP)      T3MARKER = 1282.2           (S)
       T4MARKER = 1264.1           (SKS)     T5MARKER = 826.35           (pP)
       T6MARKER = 888.28           (sP)        KZDATE = MAY 12 (132), 1990
       KZTIME = 04:50:08.900

    Apparently TauP does not include phase SPn.  (TTIMES does.)

